The Nautilus DevOps team is strategically planning the migration of a portion of their infrastructure to the AWS cloud. 
Acknowledging the magnitude of this endeavor, they have chosen to tackle the migration incrementally rather than as a single, massive transition. 
Their approach involves creating Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs) as the initial step, as they will be provisioning various services under different VPCs.

Create a VPC named nautilus-vpc in us-east-1 region with 192.168.0.0/24 IPv4 CIDR using terraform.


The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform. Create the main.tf file (do not create a different .tf file) to accomplish this task.

Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
Create the /home/bob/terraform/main.tf file with the following content to create the nautilus-vpc in us-east-1 with the 192.168.0.0/24 CIDR block:

hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_vpc" "nautilus_vpc" {
  cidr_block = "192.168.0.0/24"

  tags = {
    Name = "nautilus-vpc"
  }
}
💡 Key Details
Region: Explicitly set to us-east-1 via the provider block (the task specifies this region).

CIDR Block: Exactly 192.168.0.0/24 as required.

Name Tag: Name = "nautilus-vpc" ensures the VPC shows the correct name in the AWS console — this is what the validator checks.

Single file: Everything is in main.tf as instructed (no separate provider file).

🚀 Execution Steps
Run the following in the terminal inside /home/bob/terraform:

bash
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Verify (optional)
bash
aws ec2 describe-vpcs --region us-east-1 --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=nautilus-vpc" --query "Vpcs[].{CIDR:CidrBlock,Name:Tags[?Key=='Name']|[0].Value}"
You should see the VPC with CIDR 192.168.0.0/24 and the name nautilus-vpc.

